(load-lain "environments/catalog-discovery.lain")
(define cumulative-catalog-guide "catalog:register takes an EDN metadata string and a cell reference, and returns a receipt. Example: (catalog:register \"{:catalog/id :example/v1 :catalog/description \\\"Multiply quantity by rate\\\" :catalog/interface \\\"[quantity rate] -> amount\\\" :catalog/assumptions \\\"Compatible units\\\" :catalog/status :current :catalog/tag [:linear] :catalog/capability [[:catalog/id :cap/linear]]}\" (cell:reference f)). Use normal escaped string quotes in actual source. Registration records model claims, not proof of correctness. Repeating identical registration is idempotent; conflicting identities are rejected. catalog:resolve takes a registered keyword id and returns its LIVE CELL REFERENCE; example (define candidate (cell:value (catalog:resolve :example/v1))) then (candidate 4 3). Database persistence does not restore executable closures after restart. catalog:query takes EDN query text and EDN rules text and returns rows. Always use :in $ %. Example: (catalog:query \"[:find ?id :in $ % :where [?n :catalog/id ?id] [?n :catalog/status :current]]\" \"[]\"). Only relation queries, fact clauses and named rule calls are needed. No eval or host code. Results have at most 64 rows. Search and inspect applicability before constructing a duplicate; you may reject unsuitable candidates. Exact database schema (Datomic Local): :catalog/id is a unique keyword identity. :catalog/description, :catalog/interface and :catalog/assumptions are strings. :catalog/status is a keyword such as :current or :superseded. :catalog/tag is a many-valued keyword attribute. :catalog/capability and :catalog/depends-on are many-valued ENTITY REFERENCES, not keyword values. For example, [?root :catalog/id :cap/event-cost] binds the internal entity id to ?root; [?root :catalog/depends-on ?next] binds another internal entity id. To obtain its public keyword, join [?next :catalog/id ?next-id]. Registration lookup refs like [:catalog/id :cap/linear] resolve to those internal entity ids. All registered candidates have description/interface/assumptions/status. Do not guess alternate storage shapes. The supported subset is :find variables :in $ % :where triples and named-rule calls. No or-join, arbitrary predicates, aggregates, pull, or host functions. A transitive relation operates on entity ids. Multiple rules with the same head provide alternatives. Query only the supplied schema. The database has a :catalog/example string attribute on each operator. Retrieve it alongside description, interface, assumptions, status and identity. Examples are documentation; they do not execute and are not proof. Check them by invoking the discovered callable. Resolve a returned public id with catalog:resolve; cell:value obtains the live callable. The catalog starts with independent general-purpose operators. It can accumulate new callable registrations during this session. A composed closure can capture earlier callable values, while exposing varying domain quantities as parameters. Register it with a fresh identity, description, interface, assumptions, status, capability and a concrete :catalog/example. Do not capture the current question inputs into a supposedly general callable. Source from earlier stages is not supplied; the database is your discovery route.")
;; Examples remain database data; task instructions remain environment bindings.
(define catalog-example-guide "The database has a :catalog/example string attribute on each operator. Retrieve it alongside description, interface, assumptions, status and identity. Examples are documentation; they do not execute and are not proof. Check them by invoking the discovered callable. Resolve a returned public id with catalog:resolve; cell:value obtains the live callable. The catalog starts with independent general-purpose operators. It can accumulate new callable registrations during this session. A composed closure can capture earlier callable values, while exposing varying domain quantities as parameters. Register it with a fresh identity, description, interface, assumptions, status, capability and a concrete :catalog/example. Do not capture the current question inputs into a supposedly general callable. Source from earlier stages is not supplied; the database is your discovery route.")
(define search-examples-task "Discover operators for an event with printed pieces, labor charges, and a percentage surcharge. Query the database using the capability graph rooted at :cap/event-cost; include each candidate id, description, interface, assumptions, status and :catalog/example in rows. Include obsolete candidates for comparison. Put actual rows in discovery-results. Discovery only: do not implement the solution. The next stage must be able to assess and invoke what the database returned. Output ownership: all named output ports already belong to the parent environment. Connect them with ->; never define a local binding with the same name. Correct example: (define candidates (catalog:query query-text rules-text)) then (-> candidates discovery-results). Incorrect: (define discovery-results ...) shadows the parent port and leaves the next stage waiting. Apply the same rule to every requested output port.")
(define compose-task "Use memory-cell database results and their examples. Resolve the suitable current operators. First prove the examples by actually invoking the retained linear operator with 4 and 3, and the retained surcharge operator with 100 and 0.08; connect those computed numbers to example-linear and example-percentage. Then construct a reusable closure composing the suitable retained operators to calculate a whole event quote from five parameters: quantity, unit-price, hours, hourly-rate and surcharge-rate. Choose a fresh public identity and name, register the closure with :cap/event-cost and include a concrete numeric :catalog/example. Capture reusable callable dependencies, not the present order values. Invoke your closure on the existing input cells quantity, unit-price, hours, hourly-rate, surcharge-rate; connect the numeric answer to answer and computed text to user-output. Connect the registration receipt to cumulative-ready. Do not manually install a future solution or answer the later question. Output ownership: all named output ports already belong to the parent environment. Connect them with ->; never define a local binding with the same name. Correct example: (define candidates (catalog:query query-text rules-text)) then (-> candidates discovery-results). Incorrect: (define discovery-results ...) shadows the parent port and leaves the next stage waiting. Apply the same rule to every requested output port. Composition requirement for this test: inside the new closure, invoke the discovered linear callable for both priced quantities and the discovered surcharge callable for the surcharge. Merely testing the linear callable and then rebuilding its arithmetic in the new closure does not demonstrate composition of that dependency.")
(define search-next-task "A later event needs 200 pieces at 4 each, 10 hours of labor at 20 per hour, plus a 10% surcharge. Search the accumulated database for a whole-event quoting callable with :catalog/capability pointing directly to :cap/event-cost. Include identity, description, interface, assumptions, status and example. Connect actual query rows to next-discovery-results. Do not reconstruct a formula or refer to earlier generated binding names. This stage is discovery only. Output ownership: all named output ports already belong to the parent environment. Connect them with ->; never define a local binding with the same name. Correct example: (define candidates (catalog:query query-text rules-text)) then (-> candidates discovery-results). Incorrect: (define discovery-results ...) shadows the parent port and leaves the next stage waiting. Apply the same rule to every requested output port. Query methodology: distinguish a whole capability from its prerequisites. Here we seek the whole capability directly, so do not traverse depends-on. Generic direct lookup example: [:find ?id :in $ % :where [?cap :catalog/id :cap/example-task] [?op :catalog/capability ?cap] [?op :catalog/id ?id]], with rules text \"[]\". Add the metadata fields requested above. A public keyword is not an internal entity id: bind ?cap with the id fact before joining capability. For recursive queries too, pass the bound entity variable, never the public keyword, to entity-reference rules.")
(define apply-next-task "Use the whole-event network discovered in memory-cell, including its example and interface. Resolve that actual stored callable and invoke it with quantity-next, unit-price-next, hours-next, hourly-rate-next and surcharge-rate-next. Connect its computed numeric result to answer-next and text to user-output-next. Explain the selected catalog identity and applicability in next-notes. No earlier generated source is provided. Prefer the applicable retained whole-event closure over reconstructing its internal computation. Output ownership: all named output ports already belong to the parent environment. Connect them with ->; never define a local binding with the same name. Correct example: (define candidates (catalog:query query-text rules-text)) then (-> candidates discovery-results). Incorrect: (define discovery-results ...) shadows the parent port and leaves the next stage waiting. Apply the same rule to every requested output port.")
(define example-linear)
(define example-percentage)
(define cumulative-ready)
(define next-discovery-results)
(define quantity-next)
(define unit-price-next)
(define hours-next)
(define hourly-rate-next)
(define surcharge-rate-next)
(define answer-next)
(define user-output-next)
(define next-notes)
(define cumulative-seed (let [c0 (add-binding seed "catalog-guide" (cell:reference cumulative-catalog-guide))
c1 (add-binding c0 "example-linear" (cell:reference example-linear))
c2 (add-binding c1 "example-percentage" (cell:reference example-percentage))
c3 (add-binding c2 "cumulative-ready" (cell:reference cumulative-ready))
c4 (add-binding c3 "next-discovery-results" (cell:reference next-discovery-results))
c5 (add-binding c4 "quantity-next" (cell:reference quantity-next))
c6 (add-binding c5 "unit-price-next" (cell:reference unit-price-next))
c7 (add-binding c6 "hours-next" (cell:reference hours-next))
c8 (add-binding c7 "hourly-rate-next" (cell:reference hourly-rate-next))
c9 (add-binding c8 "surcharge-rate-next" (cell:reference surcharge-rate-next))
c10 (add-binding c9 "answer-next" (cell:reference answer-next))
c11 (add-binding c10 "user-output-next" (cell:reference user-output-next))
c12 (add-binding c11 "next-notes" (cell:reference next-notes))
] c12))
